```jsx
import React from 'react';

const Layout = ({ children, variant = 'full-width' }) => {
  return (
    <div
      className={`flex flex-col ${variant === 'full-width' ? 'w-full' : 'max-w-md mx-auto'} ${
        variant === 'full-width' ? '' : 'px-4'
      }`}
    >
      {children}
    </div>
  );
};

export default Layout;
```
In this example, we define a `Layout` component that takes two props: `children` and `variant`. The `variant` prop defaults to `'full-width'`.

We use the `className` prop to apply the styles to the component. We use the ternary operator to conditionally apply the styles based on the value of the `variant` prop.

For the full-width variant, we apply the `w-full` class to take up the full width of the container. We also apply the `flex-col` class to make the component a flex container with a column direction.

For the centered variant, we apply the `max-w-md` class to set a maximum width, and the `mx-auto` class to center the component horizontally. We also apply the `px-4` class to add some padding to the component.

You can use the `Layout` component like this:
```jsx
<Layout variant="full-width">
  <!-- content -->
</Layout>

<Layout variant="centered">
  <!-- content -->
</Layout>
```
